<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amiriya Madrasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1504">1504</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Amiriya Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Amiriya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a> is a religious school and mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada'a</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, built by the Tahirid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Amir ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> and completed in 1504. Its white domes and richly painted interiors make it one of the finest Islamic monuments of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. A long restoration project from the 1980s to 2005 rescued its decoration and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aga-khan">Aga Khan</a> Award for Architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amiriya-madrasa.jpg" alt="Amiriya Madrasa" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Embassy Yemen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amiriya_Madrasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
